course: dictionary
lesson: 230
title: Alley and its many names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An alley is a narrow lane or path between or behind buildings. When a word has more than one meaning, the words around it show you which one the writer means.
explain: «Theo was playing football in the ginnel (a narrow alley between houses) that same evening.» The clue is between houses, so a ginnel must be a narrow lane between buildings. Try the meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Alley also has other meanings. A bowling alley is a building or lane where people play bowling, and «up your alley» (informal) means the kind of thing you like. You can tell which meaning is right by asking what the words around it are about.
fact: An alley is a narrow lane or path between or behind buildings.
fact: «Dark alley» and «back alley» tell you where the alley is and often how it feels.
fact: A «blind alley» is a dead end, a path with no way through.
fact: A «bowling alley» is a building or lane where people play bowling.
fact: Regional names for a narrow passage between houses include a ginnel or snicket in the North, and a twitten in Sussex.
exercise: «When older boys followed Sam and Freddie, they did not run into an empty alley; they walked calmly into the newsagent's.» In this sentence, what is an alley? | A shop that sells newspapers and sweets | *A narrow lane between or behind buildings | A park with a little pond | A busy main road with lots of cars
exercise: «Theo was playing football in the ginnel.» What is a ginnel? | *A narrow alley between houses | A shop on the corner | A football pitch with goals | A kind of bowling lane
exercise: «Sam's party was at the bowling alley, where everyone rolled the ball down the lane.» What is a bowling alley? | *A building or lane where people play bowling | A narrow path behind houses | A kind of football pitch | A dead end street with no way out
test: Jayden says the path is a blind alley. What might that mean? | A narrow passage full of light | A path that leads straight to the park | A lane only for bicycles | *A dead end, with no way through
test: Mina said the new game was «right up her street». What does that mean? | The game was too dangerous to play | *It is the kind of thing she likes | She lost every round | The game was played in a narrow lane
exam: Safety lessons tell children to avoid a «dark alley» when they are alone; what is a dark alley? | *A dark, narrow lane between or behind buildings | A bowling lane with skittles | A bright shop with lots of people inside | A busy road with a bus stop
